window.docContents[110].resolve({"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem--The-Navier___Stokes-Millennium-Problem--Continuation-and-loss-of-regularity":{"contents":"Local existence alone does not decide global regularity. One also needs a\ncontinuation theorem identifying a norm whose boundedness prevents the\nsolution from stopping at a finite time.\n\nLet  be a trajectory in a normed space and let  be a\ncandidate terminal time. Suppose a model-specific theorem says that an\neventual boundimplies that the solution extends through . Then either  is\nnorm-unbounded as , or the solution extends. In particular,\nfailure of extension forces norm-unboundedness.The conclusion is weaker than\n: an unbounded trajectory may repeatedly return to a\nbounded region. Upgrading unboundedness to norm blow-up requires an additional\npersistence property.\n\nSplit on whether an eventual finite norm bound exists. In the bounded case,\nthe assumed continuation criterion gives extension. In the unbounded case,\nthe negation of every eventual bound is exactly norm unboundedness near the finite horizon. If\nextension is known to fail, norm-unboundedness remains.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009The Three-Dimensional Regularity Problem\u0009The Navier–Stokes Millennium Problem","header":"8.7.1.3. Continuation and loss of regularity","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem--The-Navier___Stokes-Millennium-Problem--Continuation-and-loss-of-regularity"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--The-whole-space-Gagliardo___Fourier-identity":{"contents":"For , the Fourier-side increment kernel isThe extended-real quantities gagDouble and fourierSq are respectively the\ncomplex whole-space Gagliardo seminorm squared and the homogeneous Fourier\nseminorm squared. They are kept separate from the inhomogeneous Bessel norm.\n\nThe kernel is integrable in  for : near  the character\ndiffers from one by at most a constant times\n, so the singularity is integrable when\n, while at infinity the numerator is bounded and the denominator is\nintegrable when . Homogeneity in  then reduces  to a\nsingle constant.\n\nAssume  and . The kernel is integrable in ,\n is positive for , and for every unit vector ,The constant is left as the integral  at a unit vector.\n\nNear the origin, the Fourier character differs from one by at most a constant\ntimes ; at infinity its norm is at most\ntwo. Radial power-integrability gives the two endpoint conditions. Dilation\ngives the factor , while invariance under linear\nisometries makes the remaining value constant on the unit sphere. Positivity\ncomes from an open slab on which the character is not one.\n\nWriting the double integral in terms of the increment , Plancherel\nevaluates the inner integral through the multiplier\n, and Tonelli exchanges the order of\nintegration so that the kernel profile of the previous theorem appears as the\nfactor in front of .\n\nFor , , a unit vector , and\n,The equality is in , so no finiteness assumption is hidden.\nFor a real function whose complexification lies in , the same formula\nholds for gagliardoSq, and MemGagliardo on the whole space is equivalent\nto finiteness of the homogeneous Fourier seminorm.This is the normalization of Proposition 3.4 in\n corresponding to\nMathlib's  Fourier character.\n\nRewrite the double integral using the increment . For each fixed\n, translation on  is an isometry and its Fourier transform is\nmultiplication by . Plancherel evaluates the\ninner integral, Tonelli exchanges the nonnegative  and  integrals,\nand the kernel-profile theorem supplies the factor\n. Positivity and finiteness of the named\nconstant give the finiteness equivalences.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Fourier Sobolev Spaces\u0009Fourier and Fractional Sobolev Spaces","header":"4.5.1.9. The whole-space Gagliardo–Fourier identity","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--The-whole-space-Gagliardo___Fourier-identity"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--A-heat-equation-residual":{"contents":"Return to the separated heat profile from the parabolic chapter:\n\nIt keeps its sine shape while its amplitude decays. Differentiating once in\ntime and twice in space gives the same expression,\n\nso the heat-equation residual is zero. On a rod with endpoints at  and\n, the profile also vanishes at both ends. At time zero it is the sine\nprofile. We have checked the differential identity, the boundary values, and\nthe initial value separately. A uniqueness argument would tell us whether\nanother solution with the same data is possible in the chosen solution class.\n\nThe initial-value check is essential even when the residual is exactly zero.\nFor any constant , the profile\n has the same zero residual\nand homogeneous boundary values, but its initial value differs from the\nprescribed sine by . We use this simple example to keep track\nof what a calculation has established: an equation, its data, and a\nuniqueness or stability theorem each answer a different part of the PDE\nproblem.\n\nWe have already used this kind of calculation for harmonic polynomials,\ntransport, and the rotation and shear flows. The same\ncalculator supports those proofs. For this profile we can follow the\nderivatives by eye. Longer formulas involve the same rules, with more terms\nand more conditions to track. The cas tactics\nprove supported exact identities, derivatives, and antiderivatives; cega\nproves numerical bounds from exact rational interval computations or applies\nan existing certificate, such as a Taylor or quadrature certificate with its\nanalytic hypotheses.\n\nThe fixed-point chapter gives one\nquantitative connection. For a contraction  with constant \nand fixed point , a candidate  satisfies\n. A certified defect becomes a solution-error\nbound through this stability estimate. For a PDE, the norm, boundary\nconditions, and inverse-operator estimate in that argument come from the\nanalysis of the problem, not from the residual computation.\n\nFor a real residual , the certificate relation ResidualBound R Ω ε\nmeans  for every . At\n it gives the exact equation  on that domain.\nAt positive tolerance it gives uniform control of the residual.\n\nWe justify the exact transformations with the\nsoundness of normalization theorem and\nthe numerical bounds with the\nsoundness of interval evaluation theorem.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation","header":"10.3. A heat-equation residual","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--A-heat-equation-residual"}});